<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antinoopolis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">130 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Antinoe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antinoopolis was a city founded by the emperor Hadrian in 130 CE on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-egypt">Middle Egypt</a>, near the place where his favourite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antinous">Antinous</a> had drowned. Settled with Greek colonists and given special privileges, it later became a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a>. Its ruins lie near the modern village of Sheikh Ibada.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antinoopolis.jpg" alt="Antinoopolis" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Impr. impériale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antino%C3%AB_(Antino%C3%B6polis)._Vue_de_l%27arc_de_triomphe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Antinoöpolis stood on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, nearly opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis Magna</a>, at the foot of the hill on which the village of Besa, named after the god and oracle Bes, was seated. The Via Hadriana joined it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berenice-troglodytica">Berenice Troglodytica</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> the older city on the site, Hir-we, held the great temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>, dedicated to the gods of Khmun and Heliopolis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The emperor Hadrian built the city in 130 CE on the site of Hir-we as the cult centre of Antinoüs, his deified young beloved. Earlier buildings, including a necropolis, were razed, except the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. Hadrian intended it as the first Hellenic city in the Middle Nile region, a bastion of Greek culture, and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> to settle there with incentives. To bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> into the new culture he let Bes keep his worship beside the new chief deity, Osiris-Antinoüs. The city was laid out on a grid and adorned with columns and statues of Antinoüs. Games, the Antinoeia, were held there from spring 131, with athletic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and musical contests, and were the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for several centuries.</p><p>A senate and a president governed the city, under the prefect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a>, and in 286 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nome">nome</a> of the Thebaid. It was the seat of a Christian bishop by the 4th century and a metropolitan see in the 5th. The mathematician Serenus of Antinoöpolis was born there, and in a divorce decree of 569 it was still called most illustrious. The city was abandoned around the 10th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Travellers such as Claude Sicard in 1715 and Edme-François Jomard around 1800 still saw its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, temples, a triumphal arch, colonnaded streets and a hippodrome, but in the 19th century the stone was burned for lime or used for a dam and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> factory, and the large temple was destroyed for a cement works. The site is now the village of El Sheikh Ibada. Albert Gayet's excavations uncovered a large Coptic cemetery with mummies, grave goods and thousands of textiles, and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> dug there in the years 1965 to 68.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antinoöpolis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
